**Authentication** asks *who are you?* and produces an `Authentication` object in the `SecurityContext`. **Authorization** asks *what can you do?* and checks roles/authorities against that object. Spring Security pipelines both in a filter chain.

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
@EnableMethodSecurity     // enables @PreAuthorize / @PostAuthorize
public class SecurityConfig {

    // ---- AUTHENTICATION ----
    @Bean
    public UserDetailsService users(PasswordEncoder encoder) {
        UserDetails alice = User.withUsername("alice")
            .password(encoder.encode("secret"))
            .roles("USER").build();
        UserDetails admin = User.withUsername("admin")
            .password(encoder.encode("secret"))
            .roles("USER", "ADMIN").build();
        return new InMemoryUserDetailsManager(alice, admin);
    }

    @Bean PasswordEncoder encoder() { return new BCryptPasswordEncoder(); }

    // ---- AUTHORIZATION ----
    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain chain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        return http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a
                .requestMatchers("/public/**").permitAll()                  // no auth required
                .requestMatchers("/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")              // role-based
                .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.DELETE, "/api/**").hasAuthority("SCOPE_write")
                .anyRequest().authenticated())                              // must be logged in
            .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults())                           // AuthN mechanism
            .build();
    }
}

// Fine-grained authorization on a service method
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN') or #order.ownerId == authentication.name")
    public void delete(Order order) { /* ... */ }
}
```

- The **AuthenticationManager** (invoked by filters such as `UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter`) validates credentials and returns an `Authentication` with authorities. On success it lands in `SecurityContextHolder.getContext()`.
- The **authorization layer** (`AuthorizationManager`, `@PreAuthorize`) is the *only* thing that decides whether a caller may invoke a method — URL rules are coarse, method annotations are precise, use both.
- `hasRole("ADMIN")` is sugar for `hasAuthority("ROLE_ADMIN")`. Keep the convention consistent or bugs creep in (the `ROLE_` prefix is implicit only on `hasRole`).
- Never rely on the UI hiding a button as authorization — the UI is a hint, the server is the source of truth.

All three annotations gate method calls, but they differ in expressiveness and origin.

```java
@Configuration
@EnableMethodSecurity(
    prePostEnabled = true,     // enables @PreAuthorize, @PostAuthorize (Spring)
    securedEnabled = true,     // enables @Secured             (Spring, legacy)
    jsr250Enabled  = true      // enables @RolesAllowed         (Jakarta / JSR-250)
)
public class MethodSecurityConfig {}

@Service
public class DocumentService {

    // @Secured — simple, role list only, no SpEL
    @Secured({"ROLE_ADMIN", "ROLE_EDITOR"})
    public void publish(Long id) { ... }

    // @RolesAllowed — identical semantics, JSR-250 standard, works in non-Spring EE code
    @RolesAllowed({"ADMIN", "EDITOR"})
    public void archive(Long id) { ... }

    // @PreAuthorize — full SpEL: arguments, return value, bean methods, authentication principal
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN') or (#doc.ownerId == authentication.name and #doc.draft)")
    public void edit(@P("doc") Document doc) { ... }

    @PostAuthorize("returnObject.ownerId == authentication.name or hasRole('ADMIN')")
    public Document load(Long id) { return repo.findById(id).orElseThrow(); }

    // Custom permission bean referenced via @acl
    @PreAuthorize("@acl.canDelete(#id, authentication)")
    public void delete(Long id) { ... }
}
```

| Annotation | Expression | Origin | Standout feature |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `@Secured` | role names only | Spring (legacy) | minimal, fast |
| `@RolesAllowed` | role names only | Jakarta / JSR-250 | portable across EE |
| `@PreAuthorize` | full SpEL | Spring | arguments, SpEL, beans |
| `@PostAuthorize` | full SpEL on return | Spring | filter after the fact |

- Use **`@PreAuthorize`** by default — it supports argument-level checks, bean references (`@acl.canDo(...)`), and SpEL on `authentication` and `principal`.
- Pick **`@Secured`** or **`@RolesAllowed`** only if your team prefers plain role lists or you target a non-Spring runtime.
- Enable only the flags you use (`prePostEnabled`, `securedEnabled`, `jsr250Enabled`) — each one adds an AOP advisor to every secured bean.
- Remember the proxy rule: these annotations work only on **public** methods of **Spring-managed beans**, called **through the proxy**.
